933 0 obj
  << /ActualText (RAG File v2 \024 Confrontational Insight Evaluation \050ChatGPT\051\n\n1\056 Context\nThis dataset represents a small sample of qualitative inputs related to a weather application used in Europe\056\nThe goal is not to summarise the data\054 but to\240evaluate whether a theme is strong enough to influence product decisions\056\nThe system must prioritise\240decision quality over completeness or politeness\056\n\n2\056 Raw Signals \050User Reviews\051\n\034Forecast says sunny and then it rains 10 minutes later\056 Happens all the time in spring\056\035\n\034Weather accuracy is unreliable during seasonal changes\056\035\n\034It\031s not that it\031s always wrong\054 but I don\031t trust it anymore\056\035\n\034Too many ads\054 but I could live with that if forecasts were more consistent\056\035\n\034Rain predictions are especially bad where I live \050Zurich area\051\056\035\n\034Sometimes it\031s accurate\054 sometimes completely off\056 Hard to rely on\056\035\n\034The UI is fine\054 but I check another app to confirm the forecast\056\035\n\034Feels like it doesn\031t handle local microclimates well\056\035\n\n3\056 Research Notes \050Observed Patterns\051\nUsers tolerate occasional errors\054 but not inconsistency\nTrust degrades when outcomes feel unpredictable\nSeasonal transitions increase perceived unreliability\nUsers verify forecasts using other apps \222 behavioural signal of distrust\nUI complaints are secondary to trust breakdown\n\n4\056 Evaluation Heuristics \050Use Aggressively\051\nA theme is\240STRONG\240only if\072\nIt appears across multiple signals AND\nIt shows behavioural impact \050e\056g\056 switching apps\054 verification\051 AND\nIt reveals loss of trust \050not just frustration\051 AND\nIt is specific enough to drive a product decision\nA theme is\240WEAK\240if\072\nIt is generic \050e\056g\056 \034accuracy is bad\035\051\nIt lacks behavioural evidence\nIt cannot change a product decision\nIt sounds correct but is not actionable\n\n5\056 Behaviour Rules \050Critical \024 Do Not Ignore\051\nDo NOT be polite or neutral\nDo NOT hedge \050\034might\035\054 \034could\035\054 \034possibly\035\051 unless uncertainty is the main point\nYou MUST take a clear stance\072\240STRONG \057 WEAK \057 UNCERTAIN\nYou MUST challenge the most obvious interpretation\nAssume at least one major flaw exists in every theme\nPrioritise usefulness over correctness\n\n6\056 Themes to Evaluate\nTheme A\072\n\034Users complain about weather accuracy\035\nTheme B\072\n\034Users lose trust during seasonal transitions due to inconsistent forecasts\054 leading them to verify forecasts with other apps\035\n\n7\056 Required Output Format \050Strict\051\nFor EACH theme\054 respond using\072\nVerdict\n\050STRONG \057 WEAK \057 UNCERTAIN \024 no explanation here\051\nWhy this is \050or isn\031t\051 worth attention\n\050Direct\054 specific reasoning tied to evidence\051\nWhat\031s wrong with this interpretation\n\050Actively challenge it \024 find flaws\054 bias\054 overreach\051\nWhat decision would this actually change\n\050Be concrete \024 product\054 feature\054 or direction\051\nWhat would prove this wrong\n\050Define a falsification test\051\nRefined Insight \050if salvageable\051\n\050Rewrite into something sharper \024 or explicitly reject\051\n\n8\056 Reference \024 Strong vs Weak\nStrong Insight\072\n\034Users lose trust during seasonal transitions because forecast uncertainty is not communicated\054 leading to verification behaviour across apps\056\035\nWeak Insight\072\n\034Users want more accurate forecasts\056\035\n\n9\056 Additional Instruction\nIf both themes appear valid\054 you MUST still rank them and explain why one matters more\056\nIf neither is strong\054 say so explicitly\056\nAvoid \034both are useful\035 conclusions\056)
     /K [ 93 ]
     /P 934 0 R
     /Pg 749 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

